WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:04.910
Under the sea, vision can get weird.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.780
Cuttlefish have W-shaped pupils

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.200
and mantis shrimp see many colors we don’t,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.500
but scallops have one of the weirdest visual systems of all.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:21.260
These marine creatures have up to 200 eyes,
and each of them uses a mirror,

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.040
instead of a lens to focus light.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:30.280
Researchers put these eyes under the microscope,
and found some amazing things.

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:33.989
The mirror is made from crystals of guanine.

00:00:33.989 --> 00:00:36.920
You may be more familiar with its role in
DNA,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:41.640
but crystalline guanine is also a highly reflective material

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:47.480
that can be seen everywhere
in nature, from fish scales to chameleon skin.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.820
Guanine crystals typically form into bulky prisms,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.500
but the crystals in the scallop’s eye,

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.560
form into perfect squares.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.240
Like a telescope mirror, the scallop’s tiled
squares create a smooth surface,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.860
which minimizes optical aberrations.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:11.750
The shape of this mirror is also the key to
unlocking another mollusk mystery.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:18.590
The scallop actually has two retinas, the
upper retina and the lower retina.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:23.460
The upper retina cells fire when a shadow
crosses over the scallop,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.240
indicating a potential predator attack,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.580
and tell the scallop to escape.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.380
But scientists were unsure of what exactly
the lower retina did.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.420
Using simulations, the researchers were able to infer

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.920
what happens when light hits the mirror.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:48.570
These calculations suggested that the bottom
retina provides peripheral vision in dim light.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:53.900
Of course, 200 eyes receiving light from an
area only a few inches across

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.960
would produce a lot of overlap.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:03.200
The researchers hypothesize that there may
be some process in the scallop’s ganglion

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.170
that combines this information overload into
a single cohesive image.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:11.040
But until we know how their “brain” works,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.300
the scallop’s eye will still be one of many weird optics,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.960
found beneath the sea.

